#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/id #uuid "df4f2e74-d645-5593-8b4a-9dd311c9638e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3597" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-x}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function is a radical function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n4-x\\geq 0\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad \nx\\leq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,4]$, and the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/id #uuid "13d99b42-2152-5484-b0c3-68fcb8917b85" :question/math-academy-id "e-3598" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-5x+6}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\neq 3$.\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty, 2),$ $(2,3),$ and $(3, \\infty),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/id #uuid "98c2146d-2e1f-5acc-9774-75aa200cfacb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3599" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1$ is continuous on the interval $\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/id #uuid "0cea3387-1266-5304-9239-bab53a07be8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3600" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi],$ but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at $x=0, \\pi, 2\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi),$ namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a35fd616-95e5-5092-9da6-6a7077db1376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280267} {:answer/id #uuid "f66e92d3-f6a2-5ee2-b9a2-239a366168cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{8}{5},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280268} {:answer/id #uuid "0756cfa7-0b9e-571e-9a32-337ad520acd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{8}{5},3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280269} {:answer/id #uuid "daf8c065-8cda-5b7a-9aed-a9ed6f5b62e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280270} {:answer/id #uuid "09f787a1-d112-5595-9d7c-d08667963f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{8}{5}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0756cfa7-0b9e-571e-9a32-337ad520acd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{8}{5},3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280269} :answer-field/id #uuid "17473335-7adf-53f2-8e82-88d8d5a24a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ed2a43e-3f1c-549e-b29f-a03fa40bc58f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105435" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{12-4x}+\\sqrt{5x+8}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{12-4x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,4x\\le 12\\,⟹\\,x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+8}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -8\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap [-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{8}{5},3],\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93585a8-75a9-5ba3-b1bb-082ecc16219d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280274} {:answer/id #uuid "96b86aed-efac-53c6-b0ff-c7640b9d5397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280275} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce312d7-14c3-50a4-af16-c412ac3eebf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280276} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2b14e4-2fbd-52a9-8dbc-7ce349c013e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280277} {:answer/id #uuid "f01d4938-8151-51cf-bae9-5a660cbdd3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a93585a8-75a9-5ba3-b1bb-082ecc16219d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280274} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e4238c-7266-564c-911b-137227bb39be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dccc787e-c01f-508e-8cfa-843dde59a499" :question/math-academy-id "q-105436" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4+x}+\\sqrt{3x+18}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{3x+18}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+18\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\ge -18\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-4,\\infty )\\cap [-6,\\infty )=[-4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f977f92-61c2-5d77-9797-59cec59aaa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280281} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d77bc9-e476-5003-967a-d711035cf351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282} {:answer/id #uuid "447096cc-04cb-5e9c-ab9e-d159c2038f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280283} {:answer/id #uuid "fec85f5b-e208-5c14-aa7a-1cc5c45acd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280284} {:answer/id #uuid "a27c50e0-1e3d-5267-963d-7d9ce384a7a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27c50e0-1e3d-5267-963d-7d9ce384a7a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb09c7e-c64c-5447-9bfa-fdd8873ab07b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ea16d36-3ea7-50fe-b3bc-f38bc9987502" :question/math-academy-id "q-105470" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-2)}{x-2} \\\\  & =x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 2.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0584506f-3848-5391-9347-9edae515b220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0584506f-3848-5391-9347-9edae515b220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280288} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f45990-d2b3-5b91-9e0a-38469649518f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d9b9bed-0b85-5739-9d37-0ae62917078a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d9b9bed-0b85-5739-9d37-0ae62917078a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280290} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f41689-538e-549a-b6b1-a30c0d9cab78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a214b3ea-9363-56d4-bbb3-448e85780fa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-302871" :question/problem "The largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8))$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a logarithm function, and a cosine function.\n\nThe cosine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm function.\n\nLikewise, the logarithm function $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-8>0\\,⟹\\,x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(4,\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answe