ection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}]\\cap [-3,\\infty )=[\\,-3,\\frac{4}{3}]\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ab69c8-d0a2-5d71-a580-65f6c520cf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ab69c8-d0a2-5d71-a580-65f6c520cf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280298} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46a45a9-30c5-548f-9a77-da56731e5edc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "969f9260-6344-584e-b0a9-07d3c2809ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969f9260-6344-584e-b0a9-07d3c2809ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280300} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc5063c9-2f32-5d43-adda-4eec8b46eb16" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44aee877-b04c-57e4-a227-4dc9b6aed63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44aee877-b04c-57e4-a227-4dc9b6aed63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280302} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d09453-da66-5dc2-93e3-960899e36fde" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22d7ae09-9052-5025-9576-5f8b2e36bdcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-340050" :question/problem "The largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{x-1}$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 1.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54771cb3-835d-5a21-9219-545ee23ee913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54771cb3-835d-5a21-9219-545ee23ee913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280305} :answer-field/id #uuid "2979c86f-0874-5b40-a0db-1cafc3d49bd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280304} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d73b13f0-7fd8-55b2-88b5-93c8666169b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d73b13f0-7fd8-55b2-88b5-93c8666169b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280307} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd11d4e-c8e2-5ef3-b8eb-fdaf5caa3ba7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d8b402d-9b1b-555a-8c84-f9ecd90f5024" :question/math-academy-id "q-340062" :question/problem "The largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithmic function ${log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n4+2x>0\\,⟹\\,2x>-4\\,⟹\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a72ea25-1746-531a-ad18-2cb557addd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280310} {:answer/id #uuid "7978f412-3b63-5b57-8504-0355cfd8e158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7978f412-3b63-5b57-8504-0355cfd8e158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280311} :answer-field/id #uuid "500cecee-1e7e-51ef-8505-3b96a2d943c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0988aea-a55b-59e8-b1cd-5790374b3444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0988aea-a55b-59e8-b1cd-5790374b3444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280313} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a32c16e-d62f-5cd2-b40f-7a36b3746be3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f41e2b93-5bf8-5c26-934b-94bbb5aba6a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340082" :question/problem "The largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm and a cosine.\n\nThe cosine $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function ${log}_{e}⁡(4-x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n4-x>0\\,⟹\\,x<4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bb2fd6-df39-5b8b-ba2c-5aac30617d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280316} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f89ba7-d4b0-545c-8f10-77926d08f8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280317} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d5a2d5-7078-5872-8bd4-f82a88807b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280318} {:answer/id #uuid "caea2e47-fdc7-5afd-8d35-56e6dbf7fb56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280319} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a59e84-234f-571c-9ab5-ec1cea064e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a59e84-234f-571c-9ab5-ec1cea064e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280320} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb31224-3450-514f-96af-de40fbb73b14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1d9337e-9845-5c34-ae81-3b02e3ea20aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-38831" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-x)}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{7},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 0.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f1b455-8af7-58e5-93f9-67b63e4457c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280323} {:answer/id #uuid "3323e97d-d64f-510a-93f1-a4641ebca053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280324} {:answer/id #uuid "91b5961e-007e-537c-bfc7-f7e17d1763e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280325} {:answer/id #uuid "20405749-f787-5d2c-bdb8-afb511f549a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280326} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdfd6d9-53a4-5b63-b021-e5bc3aef145e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebdfd6d9-53a4-5b63-b021-e5bc3aef145e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280327} :answer-field/id #uuid "166dc42b-b537-56d0-bd0f-277c37d5f920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1ce5ac5-2efb-579f-9d0b-24c979e96f69" :question/math-academy-id "q-39421" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(7-x).$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithmic function ${log}_{2}⁡(7-x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n7-x>0\\,⟹\\,x<7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d09e850-cdaa-52ee-ae7e-09f311aca5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280330} {:answer/id #uuid "15df15af-0aa1-5cc5-a863-450697356018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280331} {:answer/id #uuid "93e84088-5bb6-5700-9600-430c6bcb05d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280332} {:answer/id #uuid "a5912599-701b-583d-a20d-c32ca0185660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280333} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa128c4-3c89-53d9-9b3e-0c097b54a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e84088-5bb6-5700-9600-430c6bcb05d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280332} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3488713-c3f3-5a39-b0d6-1245251f8359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c540fe9-09de-52cf-ada0-ea890aa0653e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39657" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ namely\n\n$$\n[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b90fa94-1b35-5622-b357-f9af32b8787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127947} {:answer/id #uuid "95145044-5d83-5eda-9579-137bc6135130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-1)∪(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127948} {:answer/id #uuid "b7714b6f-54b0-5930-a732-b890a7a26a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127949} {:answer/id #uuid "ea58bfc3-9b73-59f5-aacd-c2e07dcbc1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127950} {:answer/id #uuid "35c3d12c-eae1-5a2a-ba8a-a661395de38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-1)∪(-1, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c3d12c-eae1-5a2a-ba8a-a661395de38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-1)∪(-1, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127951} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecca6051-ed35-5aa7-bd5d-c95b40bafebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdf79360-c3a2-5f70-b20e-0e9fca989978" :question/math-academy-id "q-39765" :question/problem "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1})$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd589af-501b-58ef-be7c-77e8aebe245d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280337} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fafe48-2b75-5c8b-982f-0d3a787bd528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280338} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ecf695-f90a-56fa-afc1-b3676f0ce3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280339} {:answer/id #uuid "daf96ed1-e2cb-583a-baae-e28322ca8c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280340} {:answer/id #uuid "47d3832d-8107-51fa-af49-98b1f281c348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf96ed1-e2cb-583a-baae-e28322ca8c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280340} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bbca804-05ba-50e4-93e4-6e760667c220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0955151-e22d-56d2-b9a3-a4df1b6955c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40081" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-\\pi ,0,\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\pi ,0)$ and $(0,\\pi ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85908d0e-b555-518b-afba-ea42a2faa2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: [- 2π, 0)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127959} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c1cae-52aa-5c12-9211-a9cdde21b742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: (-2π, - π]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127960} {:answer/id #uuid "b815a041-49d9-5c60-a2fd-0f6ce9ab2310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: [- π, 0)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127961} {:answer/id #uuid "602c8bb2-ad20-54cf-a55b-a048448795cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 2π, - π)∪(- π, 0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127962} {:answer/id #uuid "78029d4f-6e90-5fa2-b26e-25178797a468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2π, - π)∪(- π, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78029d4f-6e90-5fa2-b26e-25178797a468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2π, - π)∪(- π, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127963} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60dd2aa-59f8-535b-961c-c72a443fdbc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aab7f888-1167-50c7-9ef8-2df66ff57659" :question/math-academy-id "q-40084" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-2\\pi ,0],$ but the function $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-2\\pi ,-\\pi ,0.$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )$ and $(-\\pi ,0),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0)\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f23705fe-9d9d-504a-b057-bb092b14bc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280344} {:answer/id #uuid "b76a8d96-1039-52f2-9809-39eff5075b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280345} {:answer/id #uuid "86c2edc7-dd19-504d-9053-30f707e3edea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280346} {:answer/id #uuid "97c34894-303d-5b19-9c76-c2669dcc4156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280347} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff84efd-c029-537a-ae1b-e290370d78f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76a8d96-1039-52f2-9809-39eff5075b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280345} :answer-field/id #uuid "8871d579-e547-5578-bcad-4a40f546b6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfb018f8-cfd6-5cfc-9e2f-f66d7b913def" :question/math-academy-id "q-4561" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a98d458d-3a59-5b44-bab4-0feb0c755685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128271} {:answer/id #uuid "be8eaeb4-43f7-5514-93ae-0f7540f7dfcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-1)∪(-1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce403d9d-9b4e-5fe2-bb50-87ee7ab76ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-4)∪(-4, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128273} {:answer/id #uuid "09e2e2c8-0fce-5e52-9139-723d4f679099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 1)∪(1, 3)∪(3, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128274} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7491ca-3064-5d5c-a16b-8fafde3dd75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-2)∪(-2, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e2e2c8-0fce-5e52-9139-723d4f679099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 1)∪(1, 3)∪(3, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128274} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace33529-0e07-54fb-a78f-ec3b1c11a7c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50036c61-8854-53ce-ba5c-eb4d53ab5533" :question/math-academy-id "q-48332" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+3}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 1.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1571c51-52be-5efe-9197-53453dca2fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\infty ,-6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280351} {:answer/id #uuid "478ef610-c76e-5595-9bb0-770c7316c136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280352} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc656b9-f9a0-5a23-ade9-2baf6278b7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280353} {:answer/id #uuid "01e26da7-1453-56af-abce-8d01abe3e2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280354} {:answer/id #uuid "d267887a-3df2-5682-b1b3-8f7d5aa83049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d267887a-3df2-5682-b1b3-8f7d5aa83049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355} :answer-field/id #uuid "635c8040-ce4f-54cc-8587-0cb78f88f17f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34e23ee6-c4ef-5cbc-a4dd-fc86f69b7242" :question/math-academy-id "q-76065" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{18+3x}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function is a radical function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\sqrt{18+3x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18+3x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\ge -18\\,⟹\\, & \\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $[-6,\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c163e61-5b7a-5ab7-8d5d-5cc0c128e242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,-2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280358} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e3d4f7-589a-546b-bf31-1789f9de9c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280359} {:answer/id #uuid "d3532593-dbc4-55db-80dc-b62de47ffc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280360} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebd2b6a-0d16-5b2c-a757-34e512c2666c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280361} {:answer/id #uuid "96a9f981-ff90-5bca-bbe0-93475481d842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ebd2b6a-0d16-5b2c-a757-34e512c2666c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280361} :answer-field/id #uuid "911c23ac-f195-589e-96b9-045e84365740" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "613342e3-9443-57c2-a1f7-e3139f13e8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76067" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{6+x}+\\sqrt{5x+10}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{6+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+10}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+10\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -10\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-6,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=[-2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9958ee-c8b0-5d33-8755-e61cb445b0de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Logarithmic Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3767882e-216a-5722-a9